0	96	Androulla Vassiliou (Greek: Ανδρούλλα Βασιλείου; born 30 November 1943) is a Cypriot politician.
97	288	Between March 2008 and February 2010, she was the European Commissioner for Health, and then, until November 2014, the European Commissioner for Education, Culture, Multilingualism and Youth.
289	378	Vassiliou is very active in social and cultural fields particularly within the UN and EU.
379	483	In Cyprus she has held many important posts and is on the Board of many public and private companies.



483	525	Legal career
Vassiliou was born in Paphos.
526	748	Between 1961 and 1964, she studied law at Middle Temple (Inn of Court) in London, United Kingdom, and then between 1964 and 1966, she studied international affairs at the London Institute of World Aff
749	940	She then returned to Cyprus in 1968 to practice law while acting as legal advisor to the Standard Chartered Bank and then the Bank of Cyprus (also being on the board of various corporations).
941	1045	In 1988, she ended her legal practice when her husband, George Vassiliou, became President of Cyprus.



1045	1123	Political career

Vassiliou served as First Lady of Cyprus from 1988 to 1993.

1123	1268	Vassiliou was elected to the House of Representatives of Cyprus in 1996, for the Movement of United Democrats, and re-elected in 2001 until 2006.
1269	1389	During this time she served on the European Affairs Committee and the Joint Parliamentary Committee of Cyprus and the EU
1390	1513	She was also an Alternate Representative of the Cyprus to the European Convention which drew up the European Constitution.

1513	1671	Between 2001 and 2006, she was Vice President of the European Liberal Democrat and Reform Party, and the chairperson of the European Liberal Women's Network.

1671	1777	In February 2008, Vassiliou was nominated to succeed Markos Kyprianou as European Commissioner for Health.
1778	1982	On 3 March 2008, she took over from him in the European Commission and faced a hearing before the European Parliament in early April 2008; she was approved on 9 April 2008 by 446 to 7 with 29 abstenti
1983	2120	Under the Second Barroso Commission, from February 2010, she was given the portfolio of Education, Culture, Multilingualism and Youth.



